#### Article 38

The forms of behavioral change that can be carried out to implement Child Protection as referred to in Article 37 paragraph (2) include:  
a. developing Children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;  
b. preventing marriage at child age;  
c. caring for, maintaining, educating and protecting Children;  
d. preventing brawls between students;  
e. providing opportunities for mothers to provide exclusive breastfeeding for Children;  
f. instilling noble character and respect for parents;  
g. instilling cultural identity, local language, love for the environment; and  
h. providing special protection to Children in emergency situations, Children in conflict with the law, Children from minority and isolated groups, Children exploited economically and/or sexually, Children who are trafficked, Children who are Victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse (drugs), Children Victims of kidnapping, sale and trafficking, Children Victims of Violence both physical and/or mental, Children with disabilities, and Children Victims of abuse and neglect, Children Victims of HIV/AIDS transmission and Children Victims of social conflict.

## Part Eleven

Child-Friendly Regency Development Policy

#### Article 39

1. Child-Friendly Regency Development aims to build Regional Government initiatives that lead to efforts to transform the concept of Children's rights into policies, programs and activities to ensure the fulfillment of Children's rights.  
2. The Child-Friendly Regency Development Policy as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented based on principles that include:  
a. good governance;  
b. non-discrimination;  
c. culture and local wisdom;  
d. the best interests of the Child; and  
e. respect for the views of the Child;  
f. inclusive; and  
g. multicultural.  
3. The Child-Friendly Regency Development Policy is directed at strengthening institutions for the fulfillment of Children's rights and special protection, including:  
a. civil rights and freedoms;  
b. rights to Family environment and alternative care;  
c. rights to basic health and Child welfare;  
d. rights to education, use of leisure time and cultural activities; and  
e. rights to special protection.  
4. Further provisions regarding Child-Friendly Regency Development as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

# CHAPTER VI

## COORDINATION AND COOPERATION

## Part One

Coordination

#### Article 40

1. The Regent coordinates the protection of Women and Children against acts of Violence with:  
a. the government;  
b. regency/city regional governments;  
c. vertical agencies;  
d. educational institutions; and  
e. community institutions.  
2. Coordination as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:  
a. religious affairs;  
b. health affairs;  
c. education affairs;  
d. social affairs;  
e. legal affairs;  
f. labor affairs; and  
g. other affairs.  
3. Coordination as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus according to their authority.

## Part Two

Cooperation

#### Article 41

1. In certain cases the Regional Government may cooperate with:  
a. other regional governments;  
b. police;  
c. advocates;  
d. health workers;  
e. social workers;  
f. Companion volunteers;  
g. spiritual counselors;  
h. other law enforcers, to assist Victims in the court proceedings.  
i. national commission on Violence against Women;  
j. Indonesian Child Protection Commission or Child Protection Institution; and/ork. certain parties desired for the benefit of the Victim.  
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:  
a. socialization;  
b. provision of legal aid;  
c. health Services;  
d. Victim assistance;  
e. counseling;  
f. Social Rehabilitation;  
g. spiritual guidance; and/or  
h. repatriation and social reintegration.

# CHAPTER VII

## COMMUNITY AND BUSINESS PARTICIPATION

#### Article 42

1. Every person may participate in providing protection for Women and Children from acts of Violence.  
2. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by:  
a. reporting acts of Violence against Women and/or Children;  
b. providing first aid to Victims;  
c. independently socializing the rights of Women and Children; and/or  
d. establishing independent Community social institutions.

#### Article 43

1. Community social institutions as referred to in Article 42 paragraph (2) letter d, in implementing the protection of Women and Children against acts of Violence must coordinate with P2TP2A.  
2. The Regional Government encourages the business sector to participate in the protection of Women and Children against acts of Violence.  
3. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:  
a. socialization to employees within the company environment regarding the dangers of acts of Violence against Women and Children; and  
b. assistance as a form of corporate social responsibility.

# CHAPTER VIII

## GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

#### Article 44

1. The Regional Government conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence.  
2. Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented periodically by Regional Apparatus having affairs in the empowerment of Women and Children against acts of Violence.  
3. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:  
a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;  
b. technical guidance and training;  
c. provision of facilities;  
d. monitoring; and  
e. evaluation.  
4. Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted with the principles of:  
a. professional;  
b. transparent; and  
c. accountable.

#### Article 45

Guidance and supervision as referred to in Article 44, shall be conducted in order to realize the objectives of implementing protection for Women and Children from acts of Violence in accordance with minimum Service standards established by laws and regulations.

# CHAPTER IX

## REPORTING

#### Article 46

1. Regional Apparatus in charge of the affairs of empowerment of Women and Children from acts of Violence shall report the implementation of protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence to the Regent.  
2. Reporting as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be submitted in writing, including:  
a. administration;  
b. finance;  
c. Services; and  
d. performance.  
3. Submission of written reports as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be implemented at least once every 3 (three) months.

# CHAPTER X

## SOURCES OF FUNDS

#### Article 47

Funds for the implementation of protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence, are sourced from:  
a. Regional Revenue and Expenditure Budget; and  
b. other legitimate sources in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

#### Article 48

1. The Regional Government may provide financial assistance to Community organizations, social organizations, or non-governmental organizations and universities that implement protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence.  
2. Financial assistance as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be in accordance with the financial capacity of the Region and implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

# CHAPTER XI

## CLOSING PROVISIONS

#### Article 49

This Regional Regulation shall come into force on the date of promulgation.  

In order that everyone may know of it, the promulgation of this Regional Regulation is ordered by placing it in the Regional Gazette of South Labuhanbatu Regency.  

